cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "AetherGit: Scalable Source Control for Autonomous AI Agents"
message: "If you use this research, data, or code, please cite as below."
type: software
version: "1.0.0"
date-released: "2026-11-05"
license: MIT
abstract: |
  AetherGit is a high-throughput, always-online version control
  framework designed specifically for agentic software engineering.
  By decoupling incremental changesets from recursively computed
  derived metadata and enforcing strict client-server asymmetry,
  AetherGit eliminates repository packfiles, lock-heavy merge
  queues, and full history fetching.

  The framework is built on three formal definitions: a Repository
  State Space (DAG topology), a Recursive Derived State (Φ_D
  calculus), and a Cache Hit Probability (P(Hit) ≥ 1 − ε). It is
  specified through four architectural pillars: client-server
  asymmetry, sparse and virtualized checkouts, lock-free changesets,
  and tiered cache topology.

  The paper reports architectural benchmarks (30,000+ merges/day,
  1.5–4.0s checkout latency, 99.4% cache hit ratio) derived from
  formal analysis and comparison, not independently verified
  empirical results.
